<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!-- ============================================================================
         CRG v1 BASELINE, part 5 of 5: man_collection_outcome (A-70, M10).

         [!CONVENTION-OVERRIDE] This view exists in dcre_col and is read by
         mandates/mrg over a SECOND datasource, which breaks database-per-service:
         https://microservices.io/patterns/data/database-per-service.html. That canon
         says no service reads another service's tables, ever, including read-only,
         including through a view. Restated in ~/.claude/rules/_global/microservices.md
         section 2, and named in the banned-shapes table there.

         WHY IT IS HERE ANYWAY, ruled by the owner 2026-08-08: restore it, cut over,
         build the composition afterwards.

           - It is genuinely a COLLECTIONS view. Its body reads tx_entry, ext_tx_status
             and prg_status_class and zero mandates tables, so collections is the only
             family that can compute it. It was removed on the reasoning that "mandates
             reads it and no collections code does", which is a statement about
             CONSUMERS, not about ownership.
           - MRG does not degrade without it. CollectionOutcomeDao issues
             SELECT is_terminal_failure FROM man_collection_outcome, so an absent view
             raises "relation does not exist" on EVERY report window, not "no
             suspensions". The failure is total and per-window, and it is the reason
             the cutover cannot wait for the target design.
           - The target design below is NOT built. Restoring a known override with its
             retirement condition written down is the honest state; shipping a broken
             cutover and calling it architecture is not.

         RETIREMENT CONDITION, and it already has a home: the suspension contract in
         mrg's db/changelog/2026/07/005-man-effective.xml describes suspension deriving
         from consecutive failed collection attempts "recorded in
         man_collection_outcome in dcre_col". This override retires when that file's
         contract is restated against a projection MRG owns.

         TARGET DESIGN, recorded and not built:
           1. collections publishes the terminal-failure fact per (mandate_ref, e2e) via
              a transactional outbox
              (https://microservices.io/patterns/data/transactional-outbox.html), or an
              Open Host Service endpoint over the same projection;
           2. MRG keeps its OWN projection in dcre_man behind an anti-corruption layer
              whose client DTOs are GENERATED from the published schema, never
              hand-maintained;
           3. MRG's second datasource (ColDatasourceConfig) is deleted, which is the
              observable end of this override: while that bean exists, the override does.

         Ordering: this file runs LAST because the body resolves ext_tx_status (part 4)
         and prg_status_class (part 3) at CREATE time, and CockroachDB resolves a view
         body when the view is created rather than when it is queried.

         No bootstrap guard on tx_entry.mandate_ref, unlike the pre-v1 version of this
         file. CRR's v1 baseline creates the column unguarded
         (crr 2026/08/002-spine.xml), every DCRE database is dropped and recreated at
         the v1 cutover, and a CRG mint winning that race would crashloop the owner.
         A missing relation here fails loudly and names itself, which is what a
         dependency-ordered baseline is supposed to do.
         ============================================================================ -->

    <!-- One row per collection line (tx_entry is 1:1 with ext_tx_status), carrying its
         mandate_ref, the projected terminal status, a terminal-failure flag and the
         ingestion timestamp for "most recent N" ordering. MRG reads it BY mandate_ref
         over its read-only dcre_col datasource.

         is_terminal_failure = the status classifies as TERMINAL_NON_SUCCESS (RJCT /
         CANC, per 003-status-classification.xml). An unclassified or unknown status is
         a LEFT JOIN miss and COALESCEs to false, so only a KNOWN terminal non-success
         counts against the mandate. Sean's spec: ANY terminal non-success counts, and
         there is no reason-code filter.

         Grain: ext_tx_status is keyed by the tx identity (arrival_id, sequence), and
         its A-40 guard exists precisely because one e2e can recur across arrivals, so
         this joins on (arrival_id, sequence) and NEVER on e2e alone.

         occurred_at = tx_entry.created_at, the collection line's ingestion time.
         ext_tx_status projects no response timestamp, so this is the available
         chronological key for the sweep's DESC ordering.

         Rows with NULL mandate_ref (non-mandate collections, and every legacy V1/V2
         book, A-72) are excluded. -->
    <changeSet id="crg-v1-man-collection-outcome-view" author="dcre">
        <createView viewName="man_collection_outcome"><![CDATA[
            SELECT t.mandate_ref,
                   x.e2e,
                   x.status,
                   COALESCE(sc.classification = 'TERMINAL_NON_SUCCESS', false) AS is_terminal_failure,
                   t.created_at AS occurred_at
            FROM tx_entry t
            JOIN ext_tx_status x ON x.arrival_id = t.arrival_id AND x.sequence = t.sequence
            LEFT JOIN prg_status_class sc ON sc.code = x.status
            WHERE t.mandate_ref IS NOT NULL
        ]]></createView>
        <rollback>
            <dropView viewName="man_collection_outcome"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
